##TECHNICAL REPORT
Crypto Market Analysis Project 
1. Project Overview (Technical)
This project implements an end-to-end analytics pipeline for cryptocurrency market data using a layered architecture. The system emphasizes semantic correctness, upstream logic consolidation, and tool-appropriate responsibility separation.
Pipeline: PostgreSQL → SQL Views → Excel (ODBC) → Power BI Desktop
2. Data Architecture & Semantic Layer
2.1 Bronze Layer (Database)
Raw cryptocurrency time-series data ingested into PostgreSQL.
Base table preserves original schema and values without transformations.
Purpose: data immutability and reproducibility.
2.2 Silver Layer (SQL Semantic View)
Analytical logic centralized in a PostgreSQL view (v_crypto_analysis).
Implemented window functions (LAG) partitioned by asset and ordered by timestamp.
Derived metrics:
daily_change
price_direction
pct_change (safe division using NULLIF)
Rationale:
Prevents duplication of logic across Excel and Power BI.
Ensures consistent metric definitions across consumers.
Enables downstream tools to operate on analytics-ready data.
3. Excel Integration (Validation Layer)
3.1 Bronze Excel
Excel connected to PostgreSQL via ODBC.
Directly consumes the SQL view without local transformations.
Used to verify schema evolution and data refresh behavior.
3.2 Silver Excel (Pivot-Based Validation)
Pivot tables created for:
Coin-level aggregation
Directional analysis (Up, Down, No Change)
Confirmed correctness of SQL-derived metrics before BI ingestion.
4. Power BI Desktop Implementation
Power BI used strictly as a presentation and exploration layer.
All metrics precomputed in SQL.
Visuals limited to KPI cards, line charts, bar charts, and slicers.
No Power Query transformations or DAX-based business logic.
5. Technical Difficulties Faced
5.1 Power BI Stability & Crash Issues
Power BI Desktop crashed during Power Query access despite small dataset size.
Auto-recovery failed to restore bookmarks and navigation logic.
Required rebuilding visuals from last saved .pbix.
Skill demonstrated: resilience and recovery under unstable tooling.
5.2 Conditional Formatting Complexity (Card Visuals)
Conditional formatting for card callout values was difficult to locate.
No direct UI option to remove rules.
Required redefining numeric ranges and explicitly handling blank states.
Skill demonstrated: advanced understanding of Power BI formatting mechanics.
5.3 Inconsistent Filter Behavior (Coin Slicer)
Coin slicer intermittently displayed incomplete categories.
Debugging involved:
Visual-level filters
Page-level filters
Report-level filters
Interaction settings
Issue likely exacerbated by internal Power BI state after crashes.
Skill demonstrated: systematic filter-context debugging.
5.4 Bookmark & Button Fragility
Bookmark navigation tightly coupled with:
Visual visibility
Selection state
Filter context
Post-crash instability rendered bookmarks unreliable.
Decision taken to remove advanced navigation.
Skill demonstrated: trade-off analysis prioritizing stability.
5.5 Over-Engineering Decisions Avoided
Considered AI dashboard generators, heavy Dockerization, and advanced navigation.
Rejected due to:
Limited analytical value
Paid or cloud dependencies
Low relevance for Data Analyst interviews
Skill demonstrated: engineering judgment and scope control.
6. Technical Learning Outcomes:-
Deepened understanding of Power BI filter context and formatting rules.
Reinforced importance of SQL-based semantic layers.
Learned practical recovery strategies for BI tool crashes.
Improved discipline in avoiding unnecessary transformations and features.
Gained confidence handling real-world tooling instability.

